  'healthy': 'Healthy',
  'help_dns_body': 'The resolver that turns names into addresses, for every engine in this app.\\n\\nWhat it changes: your ISP\\\'s resolver can see every name you look up, and on many networks it also answers with the wrong address on purpose — the block you are trying to get past often starts here rather than at the connection itself. Setting a resolver of your own takes that tool away from them.\\n\\n1.1.1.1 (Cloudflare) is the default and works almost everywhere. 8.8.8.8 is Google\\\'s. 9.9.9.9 (Quad9) additionally refuses known-malicious names.\\n\\nYou can enter more than one, separated by commas — the first goes on the tunnel interface, and the whole list is handed to the transports as the resolvers to use inside the tunnel. There the resolver decides more than privacy: a CDN answers with a server near whoever asked, so a resolver in the country you exit through is what gets you a nearby node instead of one back at the gateway.\\n\\nIf pages stop loading after you change this, the resolver you typed is not reachable — put it back to 1.1.1.1.',
  'help_lan_body': 'On (the default): the proxy listens on every network interface, so another device on the same Wi-Fi or hotspot can use this phone\\\'s tunnel. Point it at this phone\\\'s local address on port %1\$d.\\n\\nWhat it is good for: a laptop or a TV with no VPN of its own, or a PC you would rather not install anything on. Tether the phone or put both on the same Wi-Fi, set the proxy on the other device, and it is covered.\\n\\nWhat it costs, stated plainly: the proxy has no password. Anyone who can reach this phone on the network can use it — and their traffic then leaves through your tunnel and counts as yours. On your own hotspot that is only the devices you tethered. On a café, hotel, airport or office Wi-Fi it is everyone on that network.\\n\\nTurn it off when you are on a network you do not control and are not deliberately sharing. With it off the proxy is reachable only from this phone.',
  'help_port_body': 'The port this app opens on your phone so other programs can send their traffic through the tunnel.\\n\\nWith the current value of %1\$d:\\n• %2\$d — the main proxy. Speaks both SOCKS5 and HTTP, so one number covers both.\\n• %3\$d — a second SOCKS listener, used only by Psiphon-over-WARP, which needs two at once.\\n• %4\$d — an HTTP proxy, published only when LAN access is on.\\n• %5\$d — the app\\\'s own status and country probe. Not for you to use, but it has to be free.\\n\\nWhen you need it: to send one program through the tunnel instead of the whole phone (turn on Proxy mode), or to let a PC on the same Wi-Fi use this phone\\\'s tunnel (turn on LAN access). In both cases you enter this phone\\\'s address and this port on the other side.\\n\\nEvery engine uses the same port, so a proxy you configure once keeps working whichever tunnel you switch to.\\n\\nChange it only if something else on the phone already holds this port. If a value is refused, the message says why.',
  'help_proxy_mode_body': 'Off (the default): the whole phone goes through the tunnel and the engine takes the network interface directly. Every app is covered without doing anything, which is what almost everyone wants.\n\nOn: the engine publishes a local proxy on the port above. What that means for the rest of the phone depends on which engine is running, and the difference is worth knowing.\n\nWith the Xray family (VLESS, VMess, Trojan, Shadowsocks, Reality) there is no device-wide tunnel at all: traffic goes through the proxy ONLY from programs you have pointed at that port by hand, and everything else keeps using your normal connection, uncovered.\n\nWith MASQUE, WireGuard and WARP-on-WARP the whole phone stays tunnelled — the traffic simply reaches the engine through that local proxy instead of through the interface. Nothing is left uncovered.\n\nThe reason to turn it on: a local proxy is the only thing another device can connect to, so this is what makes Local Network sharing possible for MASQUE, WireGuard and WARP-on-WARP.\n\nThe trap, and it only applies to the Xray family: with this on, the app can look connected while your browser is not going through it at all. If you turn it on and things stop being unblocked, that is why — the proxy is running and nothing is pointed at it.\n\nThe per-app list in Advanced VPN is the other way round and is often the better tool: it keeps the device-wide tunnel and simply leaves the apps you choose outside it.',
